---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Experiment 11.1 - Electrical Attraction and Repulsion">
    <div class="container">
        <h1>Experiment 11.1: Electrical Attraction and Repulsion</h1>
        <p class="experiment-description">Observe the electromagnetic force by investigating when electrically charged objects attract and repel one another.</p>

        <!-- Student Information Section -->
        <div class="student-info">
            <h2>Student Information</h2>
            <div class="info-grid">
                <div>
                    <label for="student-name">Name:</label>
                    <input type="text" id="student-name" placeholder="Enter your full name">
                </div>
                <div>
                    <label for="student-email">Email:</label>
                    <input type="email" id="student-email" placeholder="your.email@example.com" value="chuck@c00lnerd.com">
                </div>
                <div>
                    <label for="experiment-date">Date:</label>
                    <input type="date" id="experiment-date">
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Purpose Section -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Purpose</h2>
            <p>To observe the electromagnetic force. Specifically to observe when electrically charged objects attract one another and when they repel one another.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Materials Section -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Materials</h2>
            <ul class="materials-list">
                <li>Two balloons (Round balloons work best, but any kind will do)</li>
                <li>Thread</li>
                <li>Cellophane tape</li>
                <li>Eye protection such as goggles or safety glasses</li>
                <li>Van de Graaff generator (optional but recommended)</li>
                <li>Small metal objects (paper clips, aluminum foil pieces)</li>
                <li>Plastic rod or ruler</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- Safety Section -->
        <div class="safety-section">
            <h2>⚠️ Safety Instructions</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Wear eye protection at all times during this experiment</li>
                <li>Never point balloons at people's faces</li>
                <li>Be careful when handling balloons to avoid popping</li>
                <li>Clean up any balloon pieces immediately if balloons pop</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- Hypothesis Section -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Hypothesis</h2>
            <label for="hypothesis">Write your predictions of what will happen when objects with like charges are brought together and when objects with unlike charges are brought close together:</label>
            <textarea id="hypothesis" rows="4" placeholder="Enter your hypothesis here..."></textarea>
        </div>

        <!-- Background Information -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Background Information</h2>
            <div class="background-info">
                <h3>The Electromagnetic Force</h3>
                <p>The electromagnetic force is one of the four fundamental forces in nature. When you rub certain materials together, electrons can be transferred from one object to another, creating electrical charges.</p>
                
                <h3>Rules of Electrical Charge</h3>
                <ol>
                    <li><strong>Like charges repel one another</strong> - Two positively charged objects will push away from each other, as will two negatively charged objects.</li>
                    <li><strong>Opposite charges attract one another</strong> - A positively charged object will be attracted to a negatively charged object.</li>
                </ol>
                
                <h3>Static Electricity</h3>
                <p>Static electricity is a buildup of electrical charges within or on the surface of a material. When you rub a balloon in your hair, the balloon picks up electrons and becomes negatively charged, while your hair loses electrons and becomes positively charged.</p>
                
                <h3>Van de Graaff Generator</h3>
                <p>A Van de Graaff generator is a device that creates static electricity at a much higher voltage than simple friction methods. It uses a moving belt to accumulate electric charge on a hollow metal sphere. The generator can create charges strong enough to produce visible sparks and can cause hair to stand on end when a person touches it. It's an excellent tool for demonstrating the principles of electrostatics and the movement of electrical charges.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Procedure Section -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Procedure</h2>
            <div class="procedure-steps">
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 1: Prepare the Balloons</h3>
                    <p>Blow up the balloons and tie them off so they each stay inflated.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 2: Set Up Hanging Balloon</h3>
                    <p>Tie some thread to one of the balloons and attach the other end of the thread to the ceiling or the center of a door frame with some tape so that the balloon hangs from the thread. Make the length of the thread so that balloon one hangs at about the same height as your chest.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 3: Charge the Hanging Balloon</h3>
                    <p>Take the balloon that is hanging by the thread and rub it in your hair a little. This will cause the balloon to pick up some electrical charge. Now back away from the balloon and allow it to hang there.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 4: Test Initial Interaction</h3>
                    <p>Take the other balloon and rub it in your hair just a little. Hold this second balloon in both your hands and slowly bring it close to the balloon that is hanging from the thread. What happens?</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 5: Increase Charge</h3>
                    <p>Play with the situation a bit, trying to see what kind of motion you can induce in the hanging balloon. Record your observations in the data table.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 6: Vigorous Rubbing</h3>
                    <p>Vigorously rub the balloon that is in your hands in your hair. Spend significantly more time doing it this time compared to what you did in step 4.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 7: Test Stronger Interaction</h3>
                    <p>Once again, bring the balloon in your hands close to the balloon that is hanging on the thread. Note what happens and note how the motion of the hanging balloon compares to the motion in steps 5 and 6.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 8: Remove Hand Balloon</h3>
                    <p>Put away the balloon that is in your hands.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 9: Prepare Tape</h3>
                    <p>Take a piece of tape that is at least 15 cm long and tape it to the top of a table. Leave a little part of it unstuck, so that you can remove it in a moment. Be sure to ask your parents which table you should use for this, as what you will do in the next step can damage the finish on some tables.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 10: Charge the Tape</h3>
                    <p>Quickly rip the tape off the table and grasp it at both ends. Hold the tape near the hanging balloon, with the sticky side facing the balloon. What happens this time?</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 11: Test Tape Interaction</h3>
                    <p>Once again, play with the situation a bit to see what kind of motion you can induce in the hanging balloon. Record your observations.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 12: Van de Graaff Generator Setup (Optional)</h3>
                    <p>If you have access to a Van de Graaff generator, set it up on a non-conductive surface. Make sure it is unplugged before setup. Have an adult supervise this part of the experiment.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 13: Generator Safety</h3>
                    <p>Before turning on the generator, ensure everyone is wearing eye protection. Keep all electronic devices at least 3 feet away from the generator. People with pacemakers or other electronic medical devices should maintain a safe distance.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 14: Charging the Generator</h3>
                    <p>Have an adult turn on the Van de Graaff generator. Allow it to run for about 30 seconds to build up a charge on the metal dome.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 15: Testing with Small Objects</h3>
                    <p>Place small pieces of aluminum foil or paper clips near (but not touching) the dome of the generator. Observe what happens. Try different distances and record your observations.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 16: Testing with Hanging Balloon</h3>
                    <p>Bring the hanging balloon (from earlier steps) near the Van de Graaff generator dome without letting them touch. Observe the interaction and record what happens.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 17: Testing with Plastic Rod</h3>
                    <p>Rub a plastic rod or ruler with a piece of wool or your hair to charge it. Then bring it near the dome of the generator. Observe and record what happens.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 18: Clean Up</h3>
                    <p>Turn off and unplug the Van de Graaff generator. Clean up and put everything away.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Data Collection Section -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Data Collection</h2>
            <div class="data-table">
                <h3>Observations</h3>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Step</th>
                            <th>Action</th>
                            <th>Observations</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>4-5</td>
                            <td>Lightly charged balloon near hanging balloon</td>
                            <td><textarea class="observation-input" placeholder="Describe what you observed..."></textarea></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>6-7</td>
                            <td>Vigorously charged balloon near hanging balloon</td>
                            <td><textarea class="observation-input" placeholder="Describe what you observed..."></textarea></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>10-11</td>
                            <td>Charged tape near hanging balloon</td>
                            <td><textarea class="observation-input" placeholder="Describe what you observed..."></textarea></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>15</td>
                            <td>Small metal objects near Van de Graaff generator</td>
                            <td><textarea class="observation-input" placeholder="Describe what you observed..."></textarea></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>16</td>
                            <td>Hanging balloon near Van de Graaff generator</td>
                            <td><textarea class="observation-input" placeholder="Describe what you observed..."></textarea></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>17</td>
                            <td>Charged plastic rod near Van de Graaff generator</td>
                            <td><textarea class="observation-input" placeholder="Describe what you observed..."></textarea></td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Analysis Questions -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Analysis Questions</h2>
            <div class="questions">
                <div class="question">
                    <label for="question1">1. What happened when you brought the charged balloon close to the hanging balloon? Explain why this occurred in terms of electrical charges.</label>
                    <textarea id="question1" rows="3" placeholder="Enter your answer..."></textarea>
                </div>
                
                <div class="question">
                    <label for="question2">2. How did the interaction change when you rubbed the balloon more vigorously? What does this tell you about the relationship between the amount of charge and the strength of the electromagnetic force?</label>
                    <textarea id="question2" rows="3" placeholder="Enter your answer..."></textarea>
                </div>
                
                <div class="question">
                    <label for="question3">3. What happened when you brought the charged tape near the hanging balloon? How was this different from the balloon-to-balloon interaction?</label>
                    <textarea id="question3" rows="3" placeholder="Enter your answer..."></textarea>
                </div>
                
                <div class="question">
                    <label for="question4">4. Based on your observations, explain the two fundamental rules of electrical charge that you demonstrated in this experiment.</label>
                    <textarea id="question4" rows="4" placeholder="Enter your answer..."></textarea>
                </div>
                
                <div class="question">
                    <label for="question5">5. How did the interactions with the Van de Graaff generator differ from the balloon and tape interactions? Why was the effect stronger or weaker?</label>
                    <textarea id="question5" rows="4" placeholder="Enter your answer..."></textarea>
                </div>
                
                <div class="question">
                    <label for="question6">6. Give three real-world examples where you have experienced static electricity and electromagnetic forces in your daily life.</label>
                    <textarea id="question6" rows="4" placeholder="Enter your answer..."></textarea>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Conclusions Section -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Conclusions</h2>
            <label for="conclusions">Write a paragraph explaining your observations and how they demonstrate the electromagnetic force. Include the two rules of electrical charge in your explanation:</label>
            <textarea id="conclusions" rows="6" placeholder="Write your conclusions here..."></textarea>
        </div>

        <!-- Extension Activities -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Extension Activities</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Try the experiment with different materials (plastic combs, wool fabric, silk)</li>
                <li>Investigate what happens on humid vs. dry days</li>
                <li>Research how lightning is formed using similar principles</li>
                <li>Explore how photocopiers and laser printers use static electricity</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- Submit Button -->
        <div class="submit-section">
            <button id="submit-experiment" class="submit-button">Submit Experiment Results</button>
            <div id="submission-status" class="submission-status"></div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Load EmailJS -->
    <script type="text/javascript" src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@emailjs/browser@3/dist/email.min.js" is:inline></script>
    <script type="text/javascript" is:inline>
        // Initialize EmailJS
        (function() {
            emailjs.init("ct_s19oZHppLy9BlW");
        })();

        // Set today's date
        document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
            const today = new Date().toISOString().split('T')[0];
            document.getElementById('experiment-date').value = today;
            
            // Load saved email if available
            const savedEmail = localStorage.getItem('studentEmail');
            if (savedEmail) {
                document.getElementById('student-email').value = savedEmail;
            }
        });

        // Submit experiment results
        document.getElementById('submit-experiment').addEventListener('click', function() {
            // Get form data - only access existing elements
            const studentName = document.getElementById('student-name').value;
            const studentEmail = document.getElementById('student-email').value;
            const experimentDate = document.getElementById('experiment-date').value;
            
            // Save email for future use
            if (studentEmail) {
                localStorage.setItem('studentEmail', studentEmail);
            }
            
            // Collect all data - only access existing elements
            const observations = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll('.observation-input')).map(input => input.value);
            const conclusions = document.getElementById('conclusions').value;
            
            // Validate required fields
            if (!studentName || !studentEmail) {
                alert('Please fill in your name and email before submitting.');
                return;
            }
            
            // Extract name from email if needed
            const extractedName = studentName || studentEmail.split('@')[0];
            
            // Create comprehensive experiment data with all observations and conclusions
            const conclusionText = `
EXPERIMENT 11.1: ELECTRICAL ATTRACTION AND REPULSION RESULTS

STUDENT: ${extractedName}
DATE: ${experimentDate || 'Not provided'}

OBSERVATIONS:
1. Lightly charged balloon interaction: ${observations[0] || 'Not recorded'}
2. Vigorously charged balloon interaction: ${observations[1] || 'Not recorded'}
3. Charged tape interaction: ${observations[2] || 'Not recorded'}
4. Small metal objects near Van de Graaff generator: ${observations[3] || 'Not recorded'}
5. Hanging balloon near Van de Graaff generator: ${observations[4] || 'Not recorded'}

CONCLUSIONS:
${conclusions || 'No conclusions provided'}

EXPERIMENT SUMMARY:
This experiment demonstrates the electromagnetic force through static electricity, showing how like charges repel and opposite charges attract using balloons, tape, and Van de Graaff generator.
            `.trim();
            
            // Check for missing elements
            const missingElements = [];
            if (!conclusions) missingElements.push('Conclusions');
            const emptyObservations = observations.filter(obs => !obs || obs.trim() === '').length;
            if (emptyObservations > 0) missingElements.push(`${emptyObservations} Observations`);
            
            const missingElementsText = missingElements.length > 0 
                ? `Missing: ${missingElements.join(', ')} ❌` 
                : 'All sections complete ✅';
            
            // Show loading status
            const statusDiv = document.getElementById('submission-status');
            statusDiv.innerHTML = '<p style="color: #3498db;">Submitting experiment results...</p>';
            
            // Send email using EmailJS with proven parameter mapping
            emailjs.send('service_ot1jg6s', 'template_y556rhr', {
                student_name: extractedName,
                student_email: studentEmail,
                experiment_title: 'Experiment 11.1: Electrical Attraction and Repulsion',
                hypothesis: 'Investigating electromagnetic force through static electricity and charge interactions',
                missing_elements: missingElementsText,
                conclusion: conclusionText,
                subject: `Experiment 11.1: Electrical Attraction and Repulsion - ${extractedName}`
            })
                .then(function(response) {
                    console.log('SUCCESS!', response.status, response.text);
                    statusDiv.innerHTML = '<p style="color: #27ae60;">✓ Experiment results submitted successfully!</p>';
                }, function(error) {
                    console.log('FAILED...', error);
                    statusDiv.innerHTML = '<p style="color: #e74c3c;">✗ Failed to submit results. Please try again.</p>';
                });
        });
    </script>

    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            line-height: 1.6;
            margin: 0;
            padding: 20px;
            background-color: #f5f5f5;
        }

        .container {
            max-width: 1000px;
            margin: 0 auto;
            background: white;
            padding: 30px;
            border-radius: 10px;
            box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
        }

        h1 {
            color: #2c3e50;
            text-align: center;
            margin-bottom: 10px;
            border-bottom: 3px solid #3498db;
            padding-bottom: 10px;
        }

        .experiment-description {
            text-align: center;
            color: #7f8c8d;
            margin-bottom: 30px;
            font-style: italic;
            font-size: 18px;
        }

        .student-info {
            background: #ecf0f1;
            padding: 20px;
            border-radius: 8px;
            margin-bottom: 30px;
        }

        .student-info h2 {
            margin-top: 0;
            color: #2c3e50;
        }

        .info-grid {
            display: grid;
            grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(250px, 1fr));
            gap: 15px;
        }

        .info-grid label {
            display: block;
            font-weight: bold;
            margin-bottom: 5px;
            color: #2c3e50;
        }

        .info-grid input {
            width: 100%;
            padding: 8px;
            border: 1px solid #bdc3c7;
            border-radius: 4px;
            box-sizing: border-box;
        }

        .section {
            margin-bottom: 30px;
        }

        .section h2 {
            color: #2c3e50;
            border-bottom: 2px solid #3498db;
            padding-bottom: 5px;
        }

        .materials-list {
            background: #f8f9fa;
            padding: 15px;
            border-left: 4px solid #3498db;
            margin: 15px 0;
        }

        .materials-list li {
            margin-bottom: 8px;
        }

        .safety-section {
            background: #fff3cd;
            border: 1px solid #ffeaa7;
            border-radius: 8px;
            padding: 20px;
            margin: 20px 0;
        }

        .safety-section h2 {
            color: #856404;
            margin-top: 0;
        }

        .safety-section ul {
            margin-bottom: 0;
        }

        .safety-section li {
            margin-bottom: 8px;
            color: #856404;
        }

        .background-info {
            background: #f8f9fa;
            padding: 20px;
            border-radius: 8px;
            border-left: 4px solid #17a2b8;
        }

        .background-info h3 {
            color: #2c3e50;
            margin-top: 0;
        }

        .procedure-steps {
            counter-reset: step-counter;
        }

        .step {
            background: #f8f9fa;
            margin: 15px 0;
            padding: 15px;
            border-radius: 8px;
            border-left: 4px solid #28a745;
            position: relative;
        }

        .step h3 {
            color: #28a745;
            margin-top: 0;
        }

        .data-table {
            overflow-x: auto;
        }

        .data-table table {
            width: 100%;
            border-collapse: collapse;
            margin: 15px 0;
        }

        .data-table th,
        .data-table td {
            border: 1px solid #ddd;
            padding: 12px;
            text-align: left;
        }

        .data-table th {
            background-color: #3498db;
            color: white;
            font-weight: bold;
        }

        .observation-input {
            width: 100%;
            min-height: 60px;
            padding: 8px;
            border: 1px solid #ddd;
            border-radius: 4px;
            resize: vertical;
            box-sizing: border-box;
        }

        .questions {
            space-y: 20px;
        }

        .question {
            margin-bottom: 20px;
        }

        .question label {
            display: block;
            font-weight: bold;
            margin-bottom: 8px;
            color: #2c3e50;
        }

        .question textarea {
            width: 100%;
            padding: 10px;
            border: 1px solid #ddd;
            border-radius: 4px;
            resize: vertical;
            box-sizing: border-box;
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
        }

        textarea {
            width: 100%;
            padding: 10px;
            border: 1px solid #ddd;
            border-radius: 4px;
            resize: vertical;
            box-sizing: border-box;
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
        }

        .submit-section {
            text-align: center;
            margin-top: 30px;
            padding-top: 20px;
            border-top: 2px solid #ecf0f1;
        }

        .submit-button {
            background: #27ae60;
            color: white;
            border: none;
            padding: 15px 30px;
            font-size: 18px;
            border-radius: 8px;
            cursor: pointer;
            transition: background 0.3s ease;
        }

        .submit-button:hover {
            background: #229954;
        }

        .submission-status {
            margin-top: 15px;
            font-weight: bold;
        }

        @media (max-width: 768px) {
            .container {
                padding: 20px;
                margin: 10px;
            }
            
            .info-grid {
                grid-template-columns: 1fr;
            }
            
            .data-table {
                font-size: 14px;
            }
            
            .submit-button {
                padding: 12px 24px;
                font-size: 16px;
            }
        }
    </style>
</Layout>
